.\" mousepad.1 - manual page for Mousepad, written for the LikeOS port.
.\"
.\" Mousepad publishes no manual page of its own: upstream documents the
.\" program through its own Help menu and its .desktop entry, which is a
.\" reasonable choice for a desktop that always has a browser to hand and a
.\" poor one here, where `man' is how anything is looked up.  So this page is
.\" written rather than rendered, from the program's own option table
.\" (mousepad/mousepad-application.c), its settings schema and its appdata
.\" description -- nothing here is invented.
.\"
.\" It is installed into the port sysroot's man1 by the build driver's
.\" post_install(), so the repository's gtk3-manpages target renders it into
.\" res/man alongside every page that DID come from a source tree, and the
.\" image build ships it like any other.
.TH MOUSEPAD 1 "August 2026" "Mousepad 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mousepad \- simple text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mousepad
.RI [ OPTION "...] [" FILE "...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mousepad
is a text editor for quickly editing text files: an editor, not a development
environment and not a plugin host.  It is the graphical counterpart of
.BR nano (1)
on this system, and opens from the file manager for any file whose type is
text.
.PP
The editing area is a GtkSourceView widget, which is where most of what the
program can do comes from: syntax highlighting for some 170 languages chosen
from the
.B Document \(-> Filetype
menu or guessed from the file's name and contents, line numbers, automatic
indentation, bracket matching, a whitespace display, and unlimited undo.
.PP
Documents open as tabs in one window.  A tab can be dragged out into a window
of its own or dropped onto another window, and
.B Document \(-> Split View
shows one document twice so that two places in a long file can be edited at
once.
.PP
Text is read and written in the encoding named by
.BR \-\-encoding ;
without one Mousepad reads UTF-8 and falls back to the locale's encoding,
opening a dialog when a file is neither.  Line endings are preserved as found
and can be changed from
.BR "Document \(-> Line Ending" .
.PP
Search and replace covers the open document or every open document at once,
with plain text, whole-word or regular-expression matching, and an incremental
search bar bound to
.BR Ctrl+F .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " ENCODING" "\fR, \fP" \-\-encoding= ENCODING
Open the files that follow in
.IR ENCODING .
With an empty argument, open the encoding dialog for each file instead.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-encodings
Print the encodings this build accepts as an argument to
.BR \-\-encoding ,
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-l " LINE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-line= LINE
Put the cursor on line
.IR LINE .
A positive number counts from the top of the file, a negative one from the
bottom.
.TP
.BI \-c " COLUMN" "\fR, \fP" \-\-column= COLUMN
Put the cursor in column
.IR COLUMN .
Zero or more counts from the start of the line, a negative number from its end.
.TP
.B \-\-preferences
Open the preferences dialog rather than a document window.
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-server
Do not become, or talk to, the primary instance: this invocation gets its own
process and its own windows.  Without it, a second
.B mousepad
hands its file names to the one already running and exits, so that every
document shares one window.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quit
Ask a running Mousepad to close.  Unsaved documents are still asked about.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
The usual GTK options
.RB ( \-\-display ,
.BR \-\-class ,
.BR \-\-name ,
and the rest) are accepted as well; see
.B \-\-help\-all
for the whole list.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/Mousepad/settings.conf
Every preference the program remembers \(em window size, font, word wrap, tab
width, the toolbar and statusbar, the colour scheme.
.IP
This file is particular to this system.  Mousepad stores its settings through
GSettings, which normally writes them into the dconf database over a session
bus, and there is neither on LikeOS.  It is therefore built with the keyfile
backend, which is upstream's option for exactly that situation and which writes
this plain, editable file instead.
.TP
.I /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/
The compiled schema describing those settings.  Mousepad will not start without
it.
.TP
.I /usr/share/gtksourceview-4/language-specs/
The syntax definitions, one XML file per language, and where to add a language
that is not there.
.TP
.I /usr/share/gtksourceview-4/styles/
The colour schemes offered under
.BR "View \(-> Color Scheme" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mousepad/plugins/
Where plugin modules are loaded from.  None is built for this image.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GSETTINGS_BACKEND
Overrides the settings backend described under
.IR FILES .
Setting it to
.B memory
makes a session's changes deliberately temporary.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a file with the cursor on its last line:
.PP
.RS
.B mousepad \-l \-1 /var/log/messages
.RE
.PP
Open a file that is not UTF-8:
.PP
.RS
.B mousepad \-\-encoding=ISO\-8859\-15 notes.txt
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nano (1),
.BR pcmanfm (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Mousepad is part of the Xfce project.  This manual page was written for the
LikeOS port.
